Id: lin044i
Severity: INFO
Category: kernel
Check: check_sysctl
Fix: Set fs.suid_dumpable=0 in /etc/sysctl.d where no crash handler needs the dumps of setuid programs.
References: core(5); Linux kernel sysctl documentation (admin-guide/sysctl/fs, suid_dumpable); CIS Benchmarks for Linux distributions, 1.5 (core dumps restricted)
Controls: CIS 4.1, 10.5; NIST CM-6, SI-16; ISO 8.9; CE Secure configuration

fs.suid_dumpable is 2, "suidsafe" in core(5): a program that changed
its privilege, a setuid one above all, dumps core when it crashes,
but only through the kernel.core_pattern handler or into a file that
root alone can read; a relative core_pattern gives no dump at all.
systemd's 50-coredump.conf sets 2 wherever systemd-coredump is
installed (Debian, Ubuntu, Fedora, Arch), and tells the handler the
dump was a privileged one, so it keeps it from the user. core(5) calls
the mode appropriate where crashes are to be debugged, so it is not a
weakness; the CIS Benchmarks ask for 0 all the same, no dump of a
privileged program at all. 1 is the weakness (lin027w): the dump is
the user's to read.
  # sysctl -w fs.suid_dumpable=0
